      <Xref identifier="RFMHFOPFUZZBAD-UHFFFAOYSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="210" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="AU4FS-4, SRI, caspi">Human axillary (underarm) odor is known to be formed upon the action of bacterial enzymes on odorless precursors formed by the human body. One type of odorous compounds formed by the bacteria are sulfanylalkanols. Four such compounds have been described - |FRAME: CPD-21109|, |FRAME: CPD-21110|, |FRAME: CPD-21111|, and |FRAME: CPD-21078|. The smell of |FRAME: CPD-21110| was described as meaty and onion-like |CITS: [17191898]|. The direct precursor to |FRAME: CPD-21110|, |FRAME: CPD-21119|, was identified in axillary samples using GC-MS |CITS: [23278215]|.</Comment>
